docs/coding-practices.md:233:**Why this matters:** "Should" adds a word to every test name without adding information. It also creates a subtle framing issue -- "should return X" reads as aspirational, like a wish. "Returns X" reads as factual, like a specification. Test names are documentation of how the system behaves. They should state facts, not hopes.
docs/coding-practices.md:235:**Benefits:** Test names are shorter and scan faster in the test runner output. Reading the test suite feels like reading a specification of system behavior. And the naming convention stays consistent across the codebase, making it easy to follow the pattern when writing new tests.
docs/coding-practices.md:252:**Why this matters:** When a test fails, the first thing you see is its name. A name like "returns 14:30 for timestamp 2024-01-15T14:30:00" tells you exactly what broke without opening the test file. A generic name like "handles timestamps correctly" tells you nothing -- you have to dig into the code to understand what went wrong and what was expected.
